      <h2>Factors That Affect RCC Slab Steel Quantity</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Slab Span & Boundary Supports:</strong> Longer spans create higher bending moments requiring thicker slabs or tighter rebar spacing.</li>
        <li><strong>Single vs Double Mesh:</strong> Thick slabs or cantilever projections require top reinforcement mesh in addition to bottom mesh.</li>
        <li><strong>Clear Cover:</strong> Inadequate cover leads to rebar corrosion, while excessive cover reduces the effective structural depth of the slab.</li>
        <li><strong>Lapping & Anchorage:</strong> Spans exceeding standard 12-meter commercial bar lengths require overlap splices (typically <code>40D to 50D</code>).</li>
        <li><strong>Site Wastage:</strong> Cutting rebar creates offcuts; standard estimation accounts for <strong>3% to 5%</strong> wastage.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why Clear Cover Matters</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Clear cover</strong> is the protective thickness of concrete between the outer surface of reinforcement rebar and the outer surface of the concrete member.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Protects steel rebar from atmospheric corrosion and environmental moisture.</li>
        <li>Provides thermal insulation during building fires.</li>
        <li>Maintains proper bonding stresses between concrete aggregate and deformed rebar ribs.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For standard indoor RCC slabs, building codes (such as IS 456) typically prescribe a minimum clear cover of <strong>15 mm to 20 mm</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>Important Engineering & Safety Disclaimer</h2>
      <p>
        This online calculator is provided for educational and preliminary material estimating purposes. It does not replace formal structural analysis, deflection checks, shear capacity verifications, seismic detailing, or local building code compliance. Always consult a qualified, registered structural engineer before finalizing construction drawings or ordering steel.
      </p>
