      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            2. What is the formula for steel weight per meter?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          The formula is <code>Weight (kg/m) = D² / 162</code>, where <code>D</code> is the diameter of the steel bar in millimeters.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            3. Why is D² / 162 used for steel weight calculation?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          It is derived from the density of steel (<code>7850 kg/m³</code>) multiplied by the circular volume of a 1-meter cylinder of diameter D: <code>(π × D² / 4) × 10⁻⁶ × 7850 = D² / 162.28</code>.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            4. How do I calculate the number of bars in a slab?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Divide the distribution span (after deducting clear cover on both sides) by the center-to-center bar spacing, round up to the next integer, and add 1: <code>Number of Bars = ⌈(Span - 2×Cover) / Spacing⌉ + 1</code>.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            5. How does bar spacing affect steel quantity?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Smaller spacing (e.g., 100 mm vs 150 mm) places more bars across the span, increasing total steel weight. Spacing must be specified by the structural design to ensure adequate load resistance without causing concrete honeycomb voids.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            6. How do I calculate 8 mm steel bar weight?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          <code>8² / 162 = 64 / 162 = 0.395 kg per meter</code>. A standard 12-meter 8 mm rod weighs approximately <code>4.74 kg</code>.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            7. How do I calculate 10 mm steel bar weight?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          <code>10² / 162 = 100 / 162 = 0.617 kg per meter</code>. A standard 12-meter 10 mm rod weighs approximately <code>7.41 kg</code>.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            8. How do I calculate 12 mm steel bar weight?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          <code>12² / 162 = 144 / 162 = 0.889 kg per meter</code>. A standard 12-meter 12 mm rod weighs approximately <code>10.67 kg</code>.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            9. What is the difference between main steel and distribution steel?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Main steel directly resists bending tensile stresses and is placed along the shorter span in one-way slabs. Distribution steel runs perpendicularly to control shrinkage, distribute concentrated loads, and tie the mesh.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            10. Does this RCC Slab Steel Calculator include laps?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          You can explicitly enter a lap and anchorage allowance percentage (e.g. 5% or 10%) inside the <strong>Advanced Options</strong> drawer. The basic calculation does not silently add lap steel without user input.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            11. Does the calculator include wastage?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Cutting wastage can be configured in the Advanced Options section (typically 3% to 5% for site bar cutting).
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            12. Can I use feet and meters in this calculator?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Yes. The calculator supports toggling between <strong>Meters</strong> and <strong>Feet</strong> for slab length and width, and converts all parameters accurately.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            13. Is the calculated steel quantity suitable for final structural design?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          No. This tool is designed for material quantity estimation and budgeting. Final structural design, bar size, spacing, and development length must be approved by a qualified structural engineer.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            14. How accurate is an RCC slab steel estimate?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          When accurate bar diameters, spacing, and clear cover from engineering drawings are entered, the calculated weight closely matches the actual Bar Bending Schedule (BBS) within ±2% to 3%.
        </p>
      </details>

    </div>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>

